This post teaches the core of Next.js 16 by building one small, real app: Mini Bank. It has a dashboard with your balance, a transactions list, a detail page for every transaction, a JSON API, an "Add transaction" form, loading and error states, SEO titles, and a production build.
I built it from an empty folder in the video, one lesson per git commit. Everything on screen actually ran.
Versions: Next.js 16.3.5 · React 19.2.8 · Tailwind CSS 4 · TypeScript 5 · Node.js 24
The code below is simplified to show each idea clearly. The full walkthrough, with every file typed on screen, is in the video.
npx create-next-app@16.3.5 mini-bank --ts --tailwind --eslint --app --import-alias "@/*" --use-npm --yes
cd mini-bank
npm run dev

The flags pick TypeScript, Tailwind, ESLint and the App Router, the modern way to build Next.js apps. Install took about a minute and a half on my Mac, and the dev server was ready on localhost:3000 in under a second.
New in 16: the project includes an AGENTS.md file that tells AI coding tools to read the docs for this exact version, which ship inside node_modules/next/dist/docs.
In the App Router, a page.tsx file inside app becomes a page on your site. app/page.tsx is the homepage, and it's just a React component exported as the default export:
export default function Home() {
  return (
    <main className="mx-auto max-w-2xl p-8">
      <h1 className="text-4xl font-bold">Mini Bank</h1>
      <p className="text-gray-500">Your money, at a glance.</p>
    </main>
  );
}

Save, and the browser updates on its own.
A layout wraps your pages. Whatever you put in app/layout.tsx shows on every page, and children is the current page. When you move between pages, the layout doesn't re-render. It keeps its state, and only the page part changes.
export default function RootLayout({ children }: LayoutProps<"/">) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>
        <Header />
        {children}
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}

